WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.390
[MUSIC PLAYING] AMAH EDOH: The point is that
these events-- they didn't end in the past.

00:00:05.390 --> 00:00:10.020
The issue is that these events that happened
centuries ago, decades ago, continue to live

00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:11.020
on today.

00:00:11.020 --> 00:00:13.162
Their impacts continue to be felt.

00:00:13.162 --> 00:00:19.060
SARAH HANSEN: Today on Chalk Radio, we're
exploring transitional and reparative justice,

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:24.881
and how seeking expertise from everywhere
might lead to better understanding.

00:00:24.881 --> 00:00:27.640
AMAH EDOH: The process of reparative justice
is about how do we address that.

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:30.439
How do we address what is today?

00:00:30.439 --> 00:00:35.590
And how do we seek justice for what's happened
in the past as a means towards addressing

00:00:35.590 --> 00:00:39.309
these challenges today, and being able to
move forward from them, being able to move

00:00:39.309 --> 00:00:41.267
on to something else?

00:00:41.267 --> 00:00:43.640
SARAH HANSEN: I'm your host, Sarah Hansen.

00:00:43.640 --> 00:00:48.760
My guest today is Professor Amah Edoh, an
Assistant Professor of Anthropology and African

00:00:48.760 --> 00:00:50.710
Studies at MIT.

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:54.850
You may remember Amah from her episode on
Chalk Radio back in season one.

00:00:54.850 --> 00:00:59.910
We're sitting down with her again, this time
to discuss her course called "Reparations

00:00:59.910 --> 00:01:05.290
for Slavery and Colonization, Contemporary
Movements for Justice."

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:10.090
The inspiration for the course came in part
from the Black Lives Matter protests that

00:01:10.090 --> 00:01:12.929
took place around the world in 2020.

00:01:12.929 --> 00:01:17.620
AMAH EDOH: What was expressed during these
protests, whether in the US or in Europe--

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:22.660
where I have a lot of friends and collaborators,
and so I was hearing echoes from both sides

00:01:22.660 --> 00:01:30.880
of the Atlantic-- was kind of a framing of
contemporary racial injustice, racial discrimination,

00:01:30.880 --> 00:01:34.490
racism, as a legacy of slavery and colonialism.

00:01:34.490 --> 00:01:39.289
And so thinking about police violence, but
also thinking about housing discrimination,

00:01:39.289 --> 00:01:45.479
and also thinking about the collections that
are held in European or American museums,

00:01:45.479 --> 00:01:50.420
thinking about so many aspects of our lives
today, and recognizing that so many of the

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:55.660
disparities and the injustices that were brought
to the surface through these protests, and

00:01:55.660 --> 00:01:59.690
that we're surrounded by at all times, have
a historical origin.

00:01:59.690 --> 00:02:04.360
This is what was so powerful in these protests
around the world, was that they were saying

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:09.500
there's no dealing with what we recognize
as a problem today without dealing or talking

00:02:09.500 --> 00:02:11.610
about what happened in the past.

00:02:11.610 --> 00:02:17.129
And so this class was meant to take that seriously,
and to say, what are the models for that?

00:02:17.129 --> 00:02:21.730
Because reparations has been, and continues
to be, an incendiary topic, or still seen

00:02:21.730 --> 00:02:22.950
as somewhat controversial.

00:02:22.950 --> 00:02:28.030
And I think part of that stems from partial
understanding about what is meant by reparations,

00:02:28.030 --> 00:02:33.803
and it's kind of a knee-jerk reaction to the
idea of what reparations is.

00:02:33.803 --> 00:02:38.209
SARAH HANSEN: If you've been on any social
media site recently, Amah's statement likely

00:02:38.209 --> 00:02:39.849
feels true to you.

00:02:39.849 --> 00:02:44.939
There do seem to be strong reactions to the
idea of what reparations are.

00:02:44.939 --> 00:02:50.701
I asked Amah how she defines or conceptualize
reparations.

00:02:50.701 --> 00:02:55.489
AMAH EDOH: It's about establishing the record,
the historical record, or redressing the historical

00:02:55.489 --> 00:03:01.770
record so that the harms that have been done
can be named, and then thinking about, what

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:02.990
might justice look like.

00:03:02.990 --> 00:03:05.890
It's one thing to learn from events that have
happened in the past.

00:03:05.890 --> 00:03:11.439
It's another to try to learn from what's happening
in the moment, and put that in conversation,

00:03:11.439 --> 00:03:16.079
of course, with what's happened in the past,
but to document, learn, and interrogate what's

00:03:16.079 --> 00:03:19.510
happening as it's happening was super exciting.

00:03:19.510 --> 00:03:24.609
And the prospect of engaging students in that
process, to me, was really, really powerful

00:03:24.609 --> 00:03:26.744
as an instructor.

00:03:26.744 --> 00:03:31.140
SARAH HANSEN: Amah's interest in justice and
anthropology is something that stems from

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:32.650
her past.

00:03:32.650 --> 00:03:37.549
She shared how growing up across Western and
Southern Africa, and the United States, shaped

00:03:37.549 --> 00:03:42.204
her understanding of what it means to be Black
and African in the world.

00:03:42.204 --> 00:03:43.560
AMAH EDOH: So I was born in the US.

00:03:43.560 --> 00:03:46.260
My parents were in graduate school in the
US from Togo.

00:03:46.260 --> 00:03:49.200
And so I had an American passport from birth.

00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:53.150
And I was the only one in my family to have
an American passport, and among my friends

00:03:53.150 --> 00:03:54.450
who were in Togo.

00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:59.849
And I remember as young as, I don't know,
five or six, feeling that having this American

00:03:59.849 --> 00:04:04.029
passport was really special, and that somehow
it made me "better."

00:04:04.029 --> 00:04:06.459
It doesn't feel great to admit that, but that's
the truth of it.

00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:09.739
I felt somehow special because I had this
American passport.

00:04:09.739 --> 00:04:12.379
Why does a six-year-old have that kind of
consciousness?

00:04:12.379 --> 00:04:14.060
Where does that come from?

00:04:14.060 --> 00:04:19.450
There was always a sense that real life happened
elsewhere, that real life was happening in

00:04:19.450 --> 00:04:23.450
the US, real life was happening in Europe
and all these other places.

00:04:23.450 --> 00:04:25.910
And the goal and the idea was always to leave.

00:04:25.910 --> 00:04:30.070
And then race comes into it, when I started
paying attention to skin bleaching or even

00:04:30.070 --> 00:04:31.690
just hair straightening.

00:04:31.690 --> 00:04:33.610
These were things that I was noticing in my
environment.

00:04:33.610 --> 00:04:36.200
Whether in Togo, it was Congolese music videos.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.780
We listened to a lot of music from the Congo
at the time.

00:04:38.780 --> 00:04:42.240
And so I started noticing, or there was a
moment where you'd start noticing, men and

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:43.670
women who bleach their skin.

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:45.070
And so you could see the signs.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:46.990
And then this question about why?

00:04:46.990 --> 00:04:48.880
Why is that a thing?

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.254
And after college, I was in Zambia for a few years and...

00:04:52.254 --> 00:04:54.861
I remember one day, I tried to actually make
an experiment of it.

00:04:54.861 --> 00:04:56.659
And I was on the minibus, and I was looking
out the window.

00:04:56.659 --> 00:05:00.690
And I was, like, let me count how many women
that I see actually skin bleached, or who

00:05:00.690 --> 00:05:01.890
have evidence of skin bleaching.

00:05:01.890 --> 00:05:06.330
And it was three out of four just doing this
little ride.

00:05:06.330 --> 00:05:10.350
So this question about what does lighter skin
mean, why do people put themselves through

00:05:10.350 --> 00:05:13.210
this, given the health effects, and so on?

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:17.350
Questions about skin color, about race, about
one's place in the world-- all of these things

00:05:17.350 --> 00:05:21.914
are part of, for me, the question of Africanness
in the world.

00:05:21.914 --> 00:05:26.250
SARAH HANSEN: Amah understands that students,
too, bring their personal histories into the

00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:27.750
classroom.

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:34.070
She tries to create a space for sharing perspectives
and honoring individual experiences.

00:05:34.070 --> 00:05:40.799
And when discussing concepts like reparations,
it becomes all the more important to do this.

00:05:40.799 --> 00:05:47.810
AMAH EDOH: I think it's really about opening
up space for sharing, for conversation, and

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:52.300
honoring all the participants' contributions,
and showing that we're building this thing

00:05:52.300 --> 00:05:55.960
together as we go and learning together.

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:59.960
One thing that's evolved for me over the course
of the semester, even, was just trying to

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:03.490
shift from talking about reparations to talking
about reparative justice.

00:06:03.490 --> 00:06:06.800
I think there are associations that are made
with the word "reparations," and that can

00:06:06.800 --> 00:06:09.030
prompt knee-jerk reactions.

00:06:09.030 --> 00:06:14.400
I think that emphasizing the justice side
of it and the perceptual side of it, by saying

00:06:14.400 --> 00:06:16.740
"reparative justice," opens up a little bit
of space.

00:06:16.740 --> 00:06:21.990
But reparations or reparative justice, I think,
can be or should be part of a transitional

00:06:21.990 --> 00:06:27.930
justice mechanism, which is really just meant
to address widespread or large-scale human

00:06:27.930 --> 00:06:30.000
rights violations.

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:35.690
Reparative justice might be one of the many
things that go into seeking justice for abuses

00:06:35.690 --> 00:06:39.000
of violations that happen on a large scale,
where you can't deal with the harms that were

00:06:39.000 --> 00:06:42.363
done to people individually.

00:06:42.363 --> 00:06:47.600
SARAH HANSEN: While material compensation
is one of the most common examples of reparations,

00:06:47.600 --> 00:06:52.120
Amah explained that reparative justice isn't
just limited to that.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:56.909
Things such as amending our historical records,
and honoring the people who've been disenfranchised

00:06:56.909 --> 00:07:02.254
by colonization and racial inequity, are just
as important.

00:07:02.254 --> 00:07:07.510
AMAH EDOH: You can have material reparations
in the form of money or land or other forms

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:14.630
of resources, and then also symbolic reparations
that are, ideally, part of a healing process,

00:07:14.630 --> 00:07:20.200
a process of acknowledging that these harms
were made, that they had significant impacts,

00:07:20.200 --> 00:07:25.770
and that moving forward requires actually
addressing what's happened in the past.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:28.680
Because oftentimes, this is a critique that's
brought forward-- why do you want to reopen

00:07:28.680 --> 00:07:29.680
old wounds?

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:30.800
This is just going to stoke tension.

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:32.490
It's going to stoke conflict.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:35.250
This is only just-- it happened a long time
ago.

00:07:35.250 --> 00:07:39.250
People today are not responsible for what
was done centuries ago, et cetera, et cetera.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:42.850
But the point is that these events didn't
end in the past.

00:07:42.850 --> 00:07:47.450
The issue is that these events that happened
centuries ago, decades ago, continue to live

00:07:47.450 --> 00:07:48.450
on today.

00:07:48.450 --> 00:07:51.800
Their impacts continue to be felt whether,
it's at the level of individual bodies, or

00:07:51.800 --> 00:07:56.060
if it's at the level of societies, or in the
landscape, in the literal land.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:08:01.961
The legacies of colonialism in various parts
of the world is held in the actual land, are

00:08:01.961 --> 00:08:06.720
held in people's bodies physically, psychologically,
not to mention the structural disadvantage

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:12.170
that the descendants of enslaved people or
formerly colonized people continue to face.

00:08:12.170 --> 00:08:14.580
So these issues are not past.

00:08:14.580 --> 00:08:16.250
There's nothing that's past about them.

00:08:16.250 --> 00:08:18.140
They're very much in the present tense.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:21.060
And so the process of reparative justice is
about how do we address that.

00:08:21.060 --> 00:08:26.330
How do we address what is today, and how do
we seek justice for what's happened in the

00:08:26.330 --> 00:08:31.400
past as a means towards addressing these challenges
today, and being able to move forward from

00:08:31.400 --> 00:08:34.029
them, being able to move on to something else?

00:08:34.029 --> 00:08:39.039
That's where the transitional justice angle,
or frame comes in, or the language, let's

00:08:39.039 --> 00:08:40.039
say.

00:08:40.039 --> 00:08:45.189
Like if we think about this as a transition
towards something better, reparative justice

00:08:45.189 --> 00:08:48.126
needs to be part of that.

00:08:48.126 --> 00:08:53.180
SARAH HANSEN: One of the main assignment students
work on in the course is creating a case study

00:08:53.180 --> 00:08:57.959
documenting what it takes to work towards
reparative justice and what challenges, arise

00:08:57.959 --> 00:08:59.499
along the way.

00:08:59.499 --> 00:09:04.790
By compiling these studies, Amah is building
up an archive within her lab, the African

00:09:04.790 --> 00:09:10.740
Futures Action Lab, which she co-founded with
her colleague, Liliane Umubyeyi.

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:16.300
The goal of the Lab is to support unfolding
movements for reparative justice across Europe,

00:09:16.300 --> 00:09:19.019
Africa, and the Americas.

00:09:19.019 --> 00:09:24.160
Amah's course seeks to combine the expertise
of different activist groups, one from Algeria

00:09:24.160 --> 00:09:30.390
and one in Belgium, with those of guest speakers
by bringing them together for discussions.

00:09:30.390 --> 00:09:34.589
These dialogues are shared in the classroom,
where students can discuss them and apply

00:09:34.589 --> 00:09:36.552
them to their case studies.

00:09:36.552 --> 00:09:40.660
AMAH EDOH: Essentially, the model for the
class comes from the AFA Lab, the African

00:09:40.660 --> 00:09:45.399
Futures Action Lab, what's at the core of
our mission, which is to bring together different

00:09:45.399 --> 00:09:50.000
or to connect different forms of expertise
around these questions, recognizing that you

00:09:50.000 --> 00:09:54.120
have all these people who hold a lot of knowledge
and expertise relevant to these struggles

00:09:54.120 --> 00:09:59.110
for justice, and for racial justice, and reparative
justice, but who are not necessarily in conversations

00:09:59.110 --> 00:10:00.110
one another.

00:10:00.110 --> 00:10:03.370
So how do we facilitate these exchanges?

00:10:03.370 --> 00:10:07.200
And so bringing that in conversation with
the classroom, and saying, how can we not

00:10:07.200 --> 00:10:10.880
only bring these different folks in conversation
with one another, but how can we have that

00:10:10.880 --> 00:10:14.310
be part of the learning experience for students?

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:22.819
In the case of the Algeria group, it was made
up of three scholars and activists who are

00:10:22.819 --> 00:10:23.819
Algerian.

00:10:23.819 --> 00:10:27.959
They approached us because they were interested
in exploring what had been done, the kinds

00:10:27.959 --> 00:10:32.899
of claims that had been brought against the
French state for colonial violence broadly,

00:10:32.899 --> 00:10:37.649
and then specifically also for nuclear tests
that were conducted in Algeria in the Sahara

00:10:37.649 --> 00:10:40.100
after independence.

00:10:40.100 --> 00:10:44.749
The second group was already organized as
a civil society organization, so it was a

00:10:44.749 --> 00:10:50.930
collective of Afro-descendant Belgian people
that was formed in response to a commission

00:10:50.930 --> 00:10:56.129
that was set up by the Belgian state this
summer to examine Belgium's colonial past

00:10:56.129 --> 00:10:59.110
in the Congo, Burundi, and Rwanda.

00:10:59.110 --> 00:11:04.420
One of the speakers that we brought in was
Anna Moyo-Kupeta, who is the Executive Director

00:11:04.420 --> 00:11:08.480
of the Center for the Study of Violence and
Reconciliation in South Africa.

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:13.279
And she's a transitional justice expert, and
a lawyer, and long-time activist.

00:11:13.279 --> 00:11:20.339
She came in to speak with the Belgian group,
in particular, about how civil society can

00:11:20.339 --> 00:11:25.050
hold accountable the state commission, the
role that civil society can play in transitional

00:11:25.050 --> 00:11:26.050
justice processes.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:30.579
And it was a super interesting conversation,
a bit intimidating, I think, in the sense

00:11:30.579 --> 00:11:36.950
that she really laid out to us how this is
work that unfolds over the course of years,

00:11:36.950 --> 00:11:41.879
like the lobbying, and the advocacy work,
and the sentinel work that civil society organizations

00:11:41.879 --> 00:11:44.959
have to do to hold state entities accountable.

00:11:44.959 --> 00:11:49.560
And so it made it really concrete for the
group who could then say, what are we able

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:52.439
to do given the time and the resources that
we have?

00:11:52.439 --> 00:11:56.309
It was really important for us to have, throughout
this process, to have voices that are coming

00:11:56.309 --> 00:11:57.809
from different directions.

00:11:57.809 --> 00:12:02.499
So to have an expert from South Africa inform
the process in Belgium, for instance, was

00:12:02.499 --> 00:12:04.279
really important to us.

00:12:04.279 --> 00:12:09.490
Because again, we think about the many layers
of intervention that this course, or the work

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:12.850
that we're doing with the Lab, is meant to
do, to accomplish.

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:17.220
It's also to highlight the fact that expertise
resides everywhere.

00:12:17.220 --> 00:12:21.769
And it's not just a North-South direction
for the transfer of knowledge information,

00:12:21.769 --> 00:12:25.954
but South-South, South-North, all of it.

00:12:25.954 --> 00:12:28.310
SARAH HANSEN: Expertise resides everywhere.

00:12:28.310 --> 00:12:31.369
That's a powerful idea.

00:12:31.369 --> 00:12:34.170
And it's one that shaped how Amah designed
the course.

00:12:34.170 --> 00:12:41.480
As part of the course, she shared guest speakers'
videos on the MIT OpenCourseWare YouTube channel.

00:12:41.480 --> 00:12:47.319
She invited learners from around the world
to share their expertise, questions, and perspectives

00:12:47.319 --> 00:12:49.235
in the comments.

00:12:49.235 --> 00:12:52.809
AMAH EDOH: The idea was that these comments
could then be brought back into the classroom,

00:12:52.809 --> 00:12:57.680
and then we could, in the classroom, engage
them and be in conversation with those comments.

00:12:57.680 --> 00:12:59.910
And then that I could then bring it back.

00:12:59.910 --> 00:13:01.809
How did it actually unfold?

00:13:01.809 --> 00:13:06.470
There were a lot of comments that felt like
knee-jerk reactions to "reparations" being

00:13:06.470 --> 00:13:09.699
in the title rather than the actual content
of the videos.

00:13:09.699 --> 00:13:12.759
That was different from what we had envisioned.

00:13:12.759 --> 00:13:17.319
And so I think we had less material to work
with in terms of the comments of the videos

00:13:17.319 --> 00:13:19.749
that could be brought into the classroom than
what we had imagined.

00:13:19.749 --> 00:13:25.829
But still, it was very interesting and generative
to bring back the comments that we did get

00:13:25.829 --> 00:13:26.829
into the classroom.

00:13:26.829 --> 00:13:29.160
We had some really nice discussions with students.

00:13:29.160 --> 00:13:33.290
Because it reminded us again that these issues
that we're dealing with in the class are unfolding

00:13:33.290 --> 00:13:35.258
live.

00:13:35.258 --> 00:13:39.199
SARAH HANSEN: Amah later explained to me that
in hindsight, it might have been helpful to

00:13:39.199 --> 00:13:43.309
provide more connective tissue for the YouTube
audience.

00:13:43.309 --> 00:13:48.529
After all, students attending the MIT class
had access to readings and other course materials

00:13:48.529 --> 00:13:53.209
to provide context, and to frame the discussion
around reparations.

00:13:53.209 --> 00:13:56.040
YouTube learners didn't have those materials.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:14:00.379
To fix that, Amah is currently working on
sharing all of her teaching materials from

00:14:00.379 --> 00:14:03.170
the course on our MIT OpenCourseWare website.

00:14:03.170 --> 00:14:08.699
I found Amah's reflections interesting, so
I asked her to share three tips for other

00:14:08.699 --> 00:14:14.228
educators who might want to try opening their
classrooms through public forums.

00:14:14.228 --> 00:14:21.179
AMAH EDOH: I think having some degree of awareness
of how your topic is seen, or will land, or

00:14:21.179 --> 00:14:27.999
what it means out in the world, but remembering
what does this mean to people outside of that

00:14:27.999 --> 00:14:30.730
setting, because that has implications for
the kinds of comments that you might get,

00:14:30.730 --> 00:14:35.100
and therefore, how you might frame-- how you
share what you share, how you respond, and

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:36.379
so on.

00:14:36.379 --> 00:14:40.389
Having a great team-- I think it's absolutely
a team effort.

00:14:40.389 --> 00:14:44.649
I think working with folks who are-- again,
back to the question of expertise-- who are

00:14:44.649 --> 00:14:47.860
coming in with knowledge that we can't have
all the knowledge in ourselves.

00:14:47.860 --> 00:14:53.040
So you all brought in knowledge about these
educational platforms that open up onto the

00:14:53.040 --> 00:14:57.129
world and what it takes to make that possible
technically.

00:14:57.129 --> 00:15:01.980
And also, in terms of the nature of the engagement,
what we can expect, how to plan for that,

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:05.569
all that and all of it, really having to be
a team effort.

00:15:05.569 --> 00:15:10.579
And taking stock ahead of time of the range
of expertise that's needed to do this, both

00:15:10.579 --> 00:15:13.480
technically, content-wise, and conceptually.

00:15:13.480 --> 00:15:17.379
And then the third tip, I think, is not to
be afraid to experiment.

00:15:17.379 --> 00:15:22.059
I think a few months ago, we're just, like,
oh, god, how is this going to work out?

00:15:22.059 --> 00:15:25.720
We had the sense-- I think we all felt that
this was a good idea, and I still think it's

00:15:25.720 --> 00:15:26.720
a good idea.

00:15:26.720 --> 00:15:30.730
And I really hope to see it in the full-blown
version of the vision.

00:15:30.730 --> 00:15:32.339
Because I really still believe in the idea.

00:15:32.339 --> 00:15:33.970
I still believe in the model.

00:15:33.970 --> 00:15:36.839
I'm grateful to you all for your willingness,
also, to experiment.

00:15:36.839 --> 00:15:38.990
And I think that that's something that we
were all, let's just try it.

00:15:38.990 --> 00:15:40.949
Let's just try it and let's see what works.

00:15:40.949 --> 00:15:45.730
And I think we really did manage to-- nothing
went massively wrong.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:49.240
And actually, I think some things actually
went-- a lot actually went right.

00:15:49.240 --> 00:15:51.300
And I certainly learned a lot from it.

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:55.480
And seeing what my students produced at the
end of it, I think they got a lot from it.

00:15:55.480 --> 00:16:01.130
Activists at the end were very grateful and
also inspired by the process.

00:16:01.130 --> 00:16:02.570
So I think it was a success in many ways.

00:16:02.570 --> 00:16:06.649
So I think being willing to take risks, and
to experiment, and to recognize that it's

00:16:06.649 --> 00:16:10.370
not going to be perfect at the first pass--
this is part of learning.

00:16:10.370 --> 00:16:14.618
It's about making mistakes and figuring it
out as you go, so you can do it better next

00:16:14.618 --> 00:16:15.984
time.

00:16:15.984 --> 00:16:19.800
SARAH HANSEN: This is a philosophy that Amah
is truly living out.

00:16:19.800 --> 00:16:23.800
She shared some things she hopes to learn
from other educators to improve how teaching

00:16:23.800 --> 00:16:26.543
and learning unfold in her classroom.

00:16:26.543 --> 00:16:31.989
AMAH EDOH: I would love to learn more about
how folks talk about a topic that's considered

00:16:31.989 --> 00:16:37.559
controversial, incendiary, in a way that draws
people in-- that both does justice to the

00:16:37.559 --> 00:16:43.639
seriousness of it and that's uncompromising
in terms of its sense of what needs to be

00:16:43.639 --> 00:16:47.249
done and the importance of it, without being
alienating.

00:16:47.249 --> 00:16:53.740
How do you draw folks in without compromising
on the reality or your sense of why this matters

00:16:53.740 --> 00:16:54.740
and what it is?

00:16:54.740 --> 00:16:58.429
How do you draw people in without compromising
on that?

00:16:58.429 --> 00:17:04.260
And then, relatedly, we can't underestimate
the challenges of talking or interacting across

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:08.439
areas of practice and disciplines and expertise.

00:17:08.439 --> 00:17:09.480
We don't speak the same language.

00:17:09.480 --> 00:17:11.148
We don't think in the same terms.

00:17:11.148 --> 00:17:16.770
And so how folks have navigated that, because
building relationships takes time.

00:17:16.770 --> 00:17:23.169
So how do you foster space where folks can
interact meaningfully when they don't have

00:17:23.169 --> 00:17:26.914
very much time to spend together?

00:17:26.914 --> 00:17:31.340
SARAH HANSEN: This idea of bringing people
from different practices and disciplines together

00:17:31.340 --> 00:17:37.630
to find expertise everywhere is part of what
makes Professor Edoh's course so valuable.

00:17:37.630 --> 00:17:42.570
It creates a wealth of perspectives that are
actively reshaping how we view the conversation

00:17:42.570 --> 00:17:46.730
on race, justice, and equity around the globe.

00:17:46.730 --> 00:17:51.240
If you're interested in adding your expertise,
or to learn from others, you can find Amah's

00:17:51.240 --> 00:17:55.000
videos on our MIT OpenCourseWare YouTube site.

00:17:55.000 --> 00:17:59.429
You'll soon find her teaching materials on
our MIT OpenCourseWare website, as well.

00:17:59.429 --> 00:18:05.340
You can find Amah's lab online at www.africanfutures.mit.edu.

00:18:05.340 --> 00:18:10.100
Thank you so much for listening.

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:15.539
